1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,978
Lesson 3.5.

2
00:00:02,358 --> 00:00:04,959
Protocol design and clock acceleration.

3
00:00:06,083 --> 00:00:10,675
To solve a problem with a thermodynamic
computer you have to load it: take the

4
00:00:10,675 --> 00:00:14,795
hardware from an initial state to
the energy that encodes the problem.

5
00:00:15,176 --> 00:00:17,322
Is it better to do it
all at once or slowly?

6
00:00:17,702 --> 00:00:20,501
And if we want to go faster,
what do we have to pay?

7
00:00:20,881 --> 00:00:26,245
In this lesson we see two design tools:
gradual loading and clock acceleration.

8
00:00:27,350 --> 00:00:30,925
Let's stiffen a trap
from one to four again.

9
00:00:31,305 --> 00:00:36,533
If we do it all at once, the
particle has no time to react and the

10
00:00:36,533 --> 00:00:41,049
work is 1.5 k_B T, more than
twice the free energy, 0.69.

11
00:00:41,429 --> 00:00:47,335
If we do it over a time τ, the work
drops: 1.08 with τ equal to one

12
00:00:47,335 --> 00:00:52,535
half, 0.83 with τ equal to two,
0.73 with τ equal to eight.

13
00:00:52,915 --> 00:00:56,334
For long times, the
dissipated work decays as

14
00:00:56,334 --> 00:00:59,373
one over τ, as the speed limits predict.

15
00:01:00,483 --> 00:01:01,929
Now the inverse question:

16
00:01:02,309 --> 00:01:04,829
how do we speed up a computation
without changing what it does?

17
00:01:05,209 --> 00:01:10,190
Let's take the Langevin equation and multiply
the potential by a factor λ, together

18
00:01:10,190 --> 00:01:14,751
with the noise strength, as if we raised
the temperature by the same factor.

19
00:01:15,131 --> 00:01:19,365
If we also measure time with
a clock λ times faster, s

20
00:01:19,365 --> 00:01:23,127
= λ t, the equation is
exactly the original one.

21
00:01:23,507 --> 00:01:27,999
The trajectories are the same,
only traversed λ times faster.

22
00:01:29,117 --> 00:01:29,874
What do we pay?

23
00:01:30,254 --> 00:01:33,109
All energies are multiplied by λ, so the

24
00:01:33,109 --> 00:01:36,036
heat dissipated per operation grows by λ.

25
00:01:36,416 --> 00:01:41,955
Since the operation also takes λ times
less time, power grows by λ squared.

26
00:01:42,335 --> 00:01:45,191
But entropy production,
which is heat divided by

27
00:01:45,191 --> 00:01:48,642
temperature, does not change,
and neither does efficiency.

28
00:01:49,022 --> 00:01:53,389
The two trajectories you see are
the same noise realization: the fast

29
00:01:53,389 --> 00:01:57,567
one, with λ = 4, reaches the same
result in a quarter of the time.

30
00:01:58,683 --> 00:02:02,534
Suppose a computation dissipates
ten k_B T in one microsecond.

31
00:02:02,914 --> 00:02:06,042
With clock acceleration λ
= 10, it takes a hundred

32
00:02:06,042 --> 00:02:08,732
nanoseconds and dissipates
a hundred k_B T.

33
00:02:09,112 --> 00:02:12,099
Power goes from ten k_B
T per microsecond to a

34
00:02:12,099 --> 00:02:15,539
thousand k_B T per microsecond:
a hundred times more.

35
00:02:15,919 --> 00:02:18,370
The entropy produced is still ten.

36
00:02:18,750 --> 00:02:22,509
In real hardware, acceleration
is limited by the bandwidth of

37
00:02:22,509 --> 00:02:25,898
the components and by the
power the chip can dissipate.

38
00:02:27,017 --> 00:02:31,804
In practice, loading the problem with
a ramp reduces dissipation, and clock

39
00:02:31,804 --> 00:02:36,846
acceleration lets us trade speed for
power without changing entropy production.

40
00:02:37,226 --> 00:02:40,770
In lab four you will check
those scaling laws and look

41
00:02:40,770 --> 00:02:43,855
for the loading time that
maximizes efficiency.

42
00:02:44,983 --> 00:02:49,390
Careful: scaling only the potential
without the noise changes the distribution.

43
00:02:49,770 --> 00:02:55,792
Entropy not changing does not mean
acceleration is free: heat and power grow.

44
00:02:56,172 --> 00:02:58,491
Hardware has a finite bandwidth.

45
00:02:58,871 --> 00:03:01,347
And a gradual load still
dissipates something.

46
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:07,885
In summary: loading a problem gradually
reduces dissipation as one over τ.

47
00:03:08,265 --> 00:03:13,120
Clock acceleration scales energies
and noise by λ, and traverses

48
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:17,217
the same trajectories λ times
faster: heat grows by λ,

49
00:03:17,597 --> 00:03:22,838
power by λ squared, and entropy
production and efficiency do not change.

50
00:03:23,950 --> 00:03:29,319
To practice: explain clock acceleration,
prove the change of variables, compute the

51
00:03:29,319 --> 00:03:34,236
work of a sudden load and apply the
scaling laws with lambda equal to three.
